steps.change:
Per-chunk aligned view. Inside `for await (const raw of handle) {` in LineIndex.build, directly after the existing line `const chunkBase = totalBytes; // 本 chunk 起始的绝对偏移` (L244) and before `let i = 0;` (L245), insert exactly:

      // 字符串体 SWAR 跳过用的 4 字节对齐视图：只覆盖 buf 内完整落在 4 字节对齐位置上的字。
      // Buffer 可能是池化/subarray 切片，byteOffset 不一定对齐，头尾不足一字的字节走逐字节路径。
      const byteOff = buf.byteOffset;
      const alignOff = byteOff + ((4 - (byteOff & 3)) & 3); // 首个 >= byteOff 的 4 字节对齐偏移
      const wordCount = byteOff + n > alignOff ? (byteOff + n - alignOff) >>> 2 : 0;
      const u32 = wordCount > 0 ? new Uint32Array(buf.buffer, alignOff, wordCount) : new Uint32Array(0);

Rationale for each detail (do not deviate): (a) `(byteOff & 3)` instead of `(base + 3) & ~3` so the rounding is correct for any byteOffset (& ~3 would truncate offsets >= 2^31 to int32); (b) the view covers only bytes [alignOff, alignOff + 4*wordCount), which is always a subrange of [byteOff, byteOff + n), so it never reads outside this Buffer even when buf is a slice of a larger pooled ArrayBuffer; (c) when wordCount is 0 (chunks shorter than the alignment gap + 4, e.g. 1/2/3-byte splits or empty chunks) no view over buf.buffer is constructed, because `new Uint32Array(buf.buffer, alignOff, 0)` throws RangeError when alignOff > buf.buffer.byteLength (e.g. an empty or 1-byte Buffer at the very end of its ArrayBuffer); `new Uint32Array(0)` keeps u32 monomorphic (always Uint32Array); (d) the typed-array element load is used, NOT DataView.getUint32 (DataView SWAR measured negative, 0.92-0.98 relative). Names `byteOff`, `alignOff`, `wordCount`, `u32` must not collide with existing locals (they do not on 2fb4bdc; keep these exact names).

Replace ONLY the 4x-unrolled reject at L256-263, i.e. this statement inside `for (;;) {`:

            while (
              i + 3 < n &&
              buf[i] > 0x5c &&
              buf[i + 1] > 0x5c &&
              buf[i + 2] > 0x5c &&
              buf[i + 3] > 0x5c
            )
              i += 4;

with exactly:

            // SWAR：i 落在 4 字节对齐位置时按字跳过，直到某个字含 '"'(0x22) 或 '\\'(0x5c)。
            // haszero 逐字节判零，与字节序无关；命中字（或误报）交给下方逐字节路径精确处理。
            if (((byteOff + i) & 3) === 0) {
              let wi = (byteOff + i - alignOff) >> 2;
              while (wi < wordCount) {
                const wx = u32[wi];
                const wq = wx ^ 0x22222222;
                const wb = wx ^ 0x5c5c5c5c;
                if (((((wq - 0x01010101) & ~wq) | ((wb - 0x01010101) & ~wb)) & 0x80808080) !== 0) break;
                wi++;
              }
              i = alignOff - byteOff + (wi << 2);
            }

Everything else in the string branch stays byte-for-byte identical: the `if (escaped) { escaped = false; i++; continue; }` carry at L249-253, the comment at L254, `for (;;) {`, and the byte tail `if (i >= n) break; const sb = buf[i++]; if (sb > 0x5c) continue; if (sb === 0x22) { inString = false; break; } if (sb === 0x5c) { if (i < n) { i++; } else { escaped = true; break; } }` with its Chinese comments, plus the trailing `continue;`. Do NOT add a second u32 load per step (2-words-per-step SWAR measured 0.99), do NOT keep the old 4x byte while in addition to the word loop, do NOT add an outer non-structural skip, nextCp, quote-before-switch or any record-storage change.

Correctness notes for the executor: (1) Alignment: the guard is on the absolute position byteOff + i, so wi = (byteOff + i - alignOff) >> 2 is an exact integer >= 0 (every aligned position inside buf is >= alignOff); if wi >= wordCount (aligned position in the tail, or wordCount 0) the while does not run and i is recomputed to its own value. Unaligned head bytes and tail bytes go through the byte path; each `if (sb > 0x5c) continue;` (or a consumed non-state byte <= 0x5c) returns to the top of `for (;;)`, so the byte path walks forward until the next aligned position and re-enters the word loop on its own. (2) The word loop only advances over words with no 0x22 and no 0x5c lane. haszero has no false negatives; its only false positives are lanes above a real zero lane (borrow), i.e. only in words that already contain a hit, and any false positive just falls to the byte path. Bytes 0x00-0x21, 0x23-0x5b, 0x5d-0xff (including raw 0x0a and UTF-8 bytes) are skipped, which matches the existing byte path where they change no state. (3) JS number semantics: wx is a uint32 (may exceed 2^31); `^` yields int32; `wq - 0x01010101` may go below -2^31 as a double but the following `&` applies ToInt32 (mod 2^32), giving the exact 32-bit result; `& 0x80808080` yields an int32 compared with `!== 0`, which is correct for a negative value. (4) Escape parity: a backslash is never skipped by the word loop (0x5c lane is a hit), so every backslash is handled by the unchanged byte path, which consumes the escaped byte inline (`i++`), even when that byte lies in the next word or is a quote; after that, i may be at any alignment and the guard handles it. Runs like `\\\\\"` are processed pair by pair in the byte path exactly as before. (5) Chunk boundary: the view never extends past byteOff + n, so the word loop cannot cross the chunk end; a trailing backslash at the last chunk byte still sets `escaped = true` via `if (i < n) ... else`, and the next chunk's first byte is skipped by the unchanged carry branch before any word test. A string open across a chunk boundary continues with the new chunk's own byteOff/alignOff/u32. (6) Endianness does not matter: the test is per-lane and the exact stop byte is found by the byte path. (7) String chunks still go through the unchanged `Buffer.from(raw)`; pooled small Buffers (unaligned byteOffset inside the 8 KiB pool) are covered by (1).
